Item 13 is done, the loop is complete (13 of 13), and the gate passed.

**What I chose and why.** The reserve went to Lemma P, the two-sided pendant bound of §6. It was the next Lean target the report named (§6.5 L5), it had been brute-force checked before being stated, and §14 asked separately what it is worth on the bound-gap instances. That made it one object answering two open items in one session, with no solving.

**What was done.**
- `lean/MOSPFormalization/PendantCliques.lean` proves Lemma P for every graph, with no `sorry` and standard axioms only. A clique with q cliques of size at least t pendant at distinct vertices forces pathwidth at least t + ⌈q/2⌉ − 2. The file also has the one-decomposition form, the depth-one case behind item 03's clique-tree value, and the MOSP form on patterns.
- Before stating it, the exact Lean form was checked on 1,905 random graphs with zero violations. The maximal-clique evaluation then scored every certified corpus instance through the bound harness in 19 seconds.
- The paper gained a lemma in Appendix E and a row in Appendix A. The axiom check covers 266 theorems with 0 failures. Six new tests pass.

**The finding.** Lemma P is worth little on the record. It is above the clique floor on 554 of 6,376 instances but above the solver's bound on only 5, and above the reference on 3 of the 338 bound-gap instances, by one stack each. The bound idea is closed on this corpus, as the branching family was.

**Bookkeeping.** PROGRESS.md carries the iteration entry, the 13/13 line and `LOOP_COMPLETE`; iterations.md marks item 13 done. Nothing was committed, nothing solved, and `solutions/` was not written. The gate log is `Ralph_Loops/loop0010/gate_it13.log`.

**Left open.** F2's upper half stays on paper. Section 15 of the report, the draft `CLAUDE.md` paragraph, is for the owner to apply and should now mention Lemma P.

